     orchestrator=host.orchestrator,
     scope_runtime=host.scope_runtime, notifier=notifier)` and
     assigns to `host.dormancy`.
  4. Subscribes `comp.on_scope_event` to
     `host.scope_runtime.subscribe_all` per
     `framework/dormancy/docs/architecture.md` §"Core
     composition" — closes the Claude-API memory-system
     detection blind spot.
  5. Awaits `comp.reconcile_on_startup(orchestrator_paused=
     host.orchestrator.is_paused)` for restart edge cases.
  6. WHEN `host.memory_sidecar_url is not None`: constructs
     `MemorySupervisor` with a stdlib-urlopen-based probe
     function (mirrors the existing `memory_system` adapter's
     probe pattern; zero new third-party deps) plus an
     `on_transition` bridge that maps SupervisorTransition
     events to dormancy detector signals.
  7. Awaits `supervisor.start()` to register the background
     probe task per the supervisor's existing `start()` contract.
  8. Registers shutdown hook 'memory_supervisor' that awaits
     `supervisor.stop()` at process teardown.

Critical architectural finding (recorded at plan-authoring
time): MemorySupervisor exposes an asymmetric callback surface —
`on_recovering` (no-arg async, fires on first-good-probe after
bad), `on_normal` (no-arg async, fires on recovery-confirmed),
and `on_transition(SupervisorTransition)` (fires on every state
edge). There is NO explicit `on_degraded` callback. To detect
"memory down" using only `on_recovering` + `on_normal` is
impossible (those fire only on the recovery side). Resolution:
the bridge subscribes via `on_transition` and filters by
`t.to_state` to capture every relevant edge with one
subscription:

  to_state == degraded   → record_supervisor_signal(memory_sidecar_down)
  to_state == escalated  → record_supervisor_signal(memory_sidecar_down)  (idempotent)
  to_state == recovering → no-op  (intermediate state)
  to_state == normal     → record_supervisor_signal(memory_sidecar_recovered)

